          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Political Career in Israel</strong><br>\nAfter Israel's independence in 1948, Begin transitioned to politics, founding the right-wing Herut party. He served as an opposition leader to the dominant Labor party for many years, strongly advocating for Jewish settlement in all parts of the historic land of Israel.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Prime Minister of Israel</strong><br>\nBegin's persistence paid off in 1977 when he became Prime Minister after his Likud party won the elections. His tenure marked a shift in Israeli politics, with a focus on security and Jewish heritage.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Egyptian-Israeli Peace Treaty</strong><br>\nIn 1979, Begin and Egyptian President Anwar Sadat signed the historic Camp David Accords, leading to the Egyptian-Israeli Peace Treaty. This peace agreement was a significant achievement, as Egypt was the first Arab country to recognize Israel.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
